oming  in we want to make sure that there is a  provision to have some sort of  information around directions for use  and safety information in both official  languages and there's a variety of  different ways to do that so that's an  Avenue that's already open we've spoken  to manufacturers about the possibility  of using that pathway it's a matter of  having Supply that they can then provide  but there's nothing there's no barriers  from a regulatory perspective in terms  of bringing foreign foreign product in  thank you  I think Emily Bergeron has another  question in the room  um yes thank you  um  do you have some idea at what time the  first doses of the new vaccine that was  approved today  at what point will it be available in  vaccine centers any pharmacies okay  thank you well what I can tell you now  one vaccine  there are 10.5 million doses in the  country now and if you want to talk  about the new Pfizer vaccine  we're expecting next week to have  deliveries of doses we expect that we  have a contractors  12.6 million doses  as the weeks go by will receive a  million Doses and in total is 12.6 and  between the two yeah I think it's enough  in the meantime to cover the expected  demand for booster doses thank you and I  have another question  not sure who said  that we don't yet know  um  foreign  to that um so you're right that in terms  of the immunization strategy  Nasi has recommended that we do not need  to be prepared for a routine every  three-month program and I think Dr new  previously commented that um everyone is  very eager to have a more predictable  cycle for kova 19 boosters and and  programs  doesn't seem like we are quite there yet  the the fall program is a step towards  some predictability but  it remains to be seen you know how the  epidemiology will unfold with new  variants Etc  generally it has been taking several  months from the launch of a new vaccine  to accumulate enough evidence to be able  to ascertain vaccine Effectiveness  estimates so to understand how well the  vaccine actually performs in the real  world has been taking somewhere between  two to four months generally after the  launch of those programs I would say  overall so far throughout the pandemic  so I think we could anticipate that  Within  several months we will start  understanding vaccine Effectiveness real  world evidence to see the performance of  the product  and then over time that can be monitored  and you know measured against the  emergence of new variants and all of  those considerations will have to factor  in we understand that the manufacturers  are continuing to consider what the  forward path for their products will be  and if they might generate new boosters  that's still a bit unclear so I think  there will be probably regulatory  Frameworks that will emerge and Dr  Sharma could maybe comment on that about  you know regulatory Frameworks around  future cycles for other booster products  but at this point we're a bit in a  weight in c space we are launching this  fall program and then we'll monitor over  time but we don't anticipate routine  every three months it's not a  sustainable immunization program model  it doesn't exist in any other areas and  it just doesn't seem like a realistic  program so although there are some  exceptional circumstances where these  shorter intervals may be necessary we're  going to be looking towards some kind of  a more sustainable approach and so nasty  has been clearing their advice on that  that we'll have to keep watching and  develop some more sustainable long-term  framework well as they say doctor in  potatoes thank you and perhaps I could  add something in French  and we have a lot more experiences with  other infectious diseases such as the  seasonal flu so I think we are still  learning and we continue to monitor the  evolution of the virus and of course in  the future we may find some pace for a  booster dose foreign  maybe it's a very short limit but the  gener